<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The pilot build (NFR-7): a folder a colleague can copy and run.

  Invoked as:
      dotnet publish src/Ims.App -c Release /p:PublishProfile=Pilot

  Self-contained on purpose, which is the one decision here worth explaining.
  NFR-7 asks that IMS install and update without per-user local administrator
  rights, and a framework-dependent build does not clear that bar: it needs the
  .NET 9 desktop runtime present, and installing that is an administrator action
  on a managed workstation. Carrying the runtime in the folder trades roughly
  150 MB for a copy-and-run install, which is the right way round for a pilot
  measured in a handful of users.

  What is deliberately NOT bundled is the IBM Client SDK. DEC-10 keeps a product
  or open-source release possible and so forbids redistributing IBM's client
  libraries; NFR-6 makes the CSDK a stated prerequisite instead. IMS checks for
  it at startup and reports its absence as a prerequisite failure rather than a
  connection error (PR-1.8).

  win-x64 is a requirement, not a preference. A 64-bit process cannot load the
  32-bit Informix ODBC driver, and CsdkLocator says so explicitly when it finds
  only the 32-bit one registered.

  Single-file is off. It buys a tidier folder and costs a worse first-run
  experience — extraction to a temp directory, slower start, and a harder time
  for anyone diagnosing a load failure against the ODBC driver, which is the
  most likely thing to go wrong on a machine that is not the author's.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Configuration>Release</Configuration>
    <Platform>Any CPU</Platform>
    <PublishDir>..\..\publish\pilot\</PublishDir>
    <PublishProtocol>FileSystem</PublishProtocol>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishSingleFile>false</PublishSingleFile>
    <PublishReadyToRun>false</PublishReadyToRun>

    <!--
      Trimming is off and must stay off. WPF does not support it, and the
      configuration binder and logging providers resolve types by reflection —
      a trimmed build would fail at runtime, on someone else's machine, with an
      error that points nowhere near the cause.
    -->
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>

    <!-- A pilot build people will report bugs against needs readable stacks. -->
    <DebugType>portable</DebugType>
    <DebugSymbols>true</DebugSymbols>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
